// hello.tr — the smallest interesting program in Trust.
//
// The message is a string literal, which is a `&'static str`: a pointer to
// static storage and a length in *characters*. Text here is one character per
// word, fixed width (Ch. 5 §1.1), so `s.len()` counts characters and `s[i]`
// is one — both O(1), and neither is available in a language whose strings
// are UTF-8.
//
// The price is paid at the boundary instead. The machine's character port
// takes UTF-8 code units (ISA §2.2), so `print` encodes on the way out — and
// that is the only place a program's text meets the interchange format. It is
// in the prelude, written in Trust, and so is everything it calls except
// `putchar`, which reaches a device and therefore cannot be (Ch. 5 §1.5).

fn main() -> t27 {
    println("Hello, 世界! 🙂");
    0
}
